  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nToday through Wednesday... Surface high pressure and ridging \naloft will be the story of our weather this weekend into the \nfirst half of the next week. This morning the high pressure is \nkeeping skies clear and allowing for ample radiational cooling. \nThis is bring temperatures down into the upper teens to lower \n20s with calm winds. This is because the high pressure is more \nor less right overhead today. Mostly sunny conditions today, as \nexpected with the high, will warm us up to near normal (now in \nthe lower 40s for highs). This surface high will begin to slowly\ntravel to the east coast this week. As the high moves to the \neast this will allow us to move more into a southerly flow, \nbringing in warmer air. For this reason Saturday night should be\nwarmer across western Minnesota, but just as cold across \nwestern Wisconsin. The flow will continue to evolve as \neverything shifts east such that we warm slightly each day over \nthe next week peaking mid week. Current guidance favors \nWednesday as this peak with widespread highs in the 50s which is\n10 to 15 degrees above normal. \n\nThursday into Next Weekend... The previously mentioned peak \noccurs because a pattern shift is starting to look likely late \nweek, possibly on Thursday. This would be with a shortwave \nbringing a chance for rain and some cooler air (most likely in\ncentral Minnesota). Looking farther out there is agreement \nwithin global models, ensemble systems, and machine learning \nguidance that a larger system could move across CONUS a week \nfrom now. Still a fair amount of spread on how this system\nevolves. Despite this spread this does look like it would stay \nwarm enough for rain rather than snow at this point. Track wise \nsurprisingly good agreement this far out indicating the Upper \nMidwest could see this rain. This has enough for NBM to have \nlikely PoPs, which isn't common this far out. This track will \nalso impact how temperatures end up as locations in this \nsystem's warm sector will see continued 50s with the cooler air \nin the cool sector. This has the NBM interquartile range a week \nfrom now for the Twin Cities, as an example, ranging from 47 to \n61 for high temperatures.",
